  "body": "## What changed\nThis is the follow-up to #402 / PR #414. Two more places drew XY arcs (G2/G3 in G17) as chords inside the arc: the **field engine** (`field_cut::chords`) and the **envelope scan** that runs while toolpaths are generated (`stock_sim/scan.rs`). Both now read the true circle.\n\n**Field engine:** new `crates/sim/src/stock_sim/field_arc.rs`, 284 lines, with a provenance header.\n- An upright mill tool's XY arc is now cut as `Sweep::Arc` pieces.\n- Pieces follow the old chord budget, capped at a quarter turn. So the lattice windows and the `MAX_CELLS_PER_MOVE` limit refuse exactly what they refused before. Each window grows by its piece's sagitta.\n- **Level piece:** solved exactly in closed form. The nearest tool pose is at the point's own bearing, clamped to the piece. It uses the column engine's tie rules (`flat_tie`, `bull_tie`), and `field_ray` gets the exact floor plane from it.\n- **Helical piece:** searched with `dexel_arc::piece_min`, #414's search (samples, then a golden section). Every result is a real distance evaluation plus the usual 1e-12 margin, so the field never shows material removed that the tool did not cut. Its `residual` is a proven bound: half a sample step of tool travel.\n- Tilted tools, lathe tools and XZ/YZ arcs keep their chords.\n\n**Envelope scan:** new `crates/sim/src/stock_sim/scan_arc.rs`, 146 lines.\n- Each arc piece keeps the chord `arc_chords` drew. That chord is now only used for indexing (bins, boxes, ring ids, the ridge law's pass counting), so all of that is unchanged.\n- Every distance the verdict uses is now measured to the arc:\n  - point distances for rest, flank and hidden rest (exact for level arcs);\n  - facet distances for gouge, searched only when the chord is within the piece's bulge of the ball.\n- The gouge bin walk, the ridge boxes and the `SegGrid` ring prune each grow by the bulge (`sag + |dz|`), so no contact the chords caught can be missed.\n\n**Tests tightened:** `stock_bull_nose::both_engines_cut_a_bull_nose_alike` now holds the helix arc to the same 1e-5 engine parity as straight moves, and the `arc_parity` relaxation is removed.\n\nThe decisions and the dropped alternatives (straddling chords, finer chords, one whole-arc sweep) are in `docs/decisions/413-arc-chords-field.md`.\n\n## Pinning tests\n- **New `sim/tests/field_arc_sweep.rs`** (5 tests; 4 fail on the old code):\n  - `the_field_cuts_every_tool_on_the_true_arc_like_the_column`: flat, ball, cone and bull tools on level and helical arcs, CW and CCW, including a sweep past half a turn and one with a radius smaller than the tool. The field's top matches the column engine's to 1e-5.\n  - `the_bull_helix_rim_is_cut_on_the_field_too`: the #414 helix (bull r 2, corner 0.75). Rim columns are cut more than 2 mm deep, matching the column engine to 1e-5.\n  - `a_clean_convex_corner_arc_reads_no_gouge_on_the_field`: a column 0.06 mm inside a riser corner. The old chords cut it 10 mm deep, top 28 → 15. Now it is untouched and reads no gouge.\n  - `a_convex_corner_arc_cut_too_tight_is_still_a_gouge_on_the_field`: a planted twin that is still convicted at 10 mm.\n  - `a_concave_fillet_wall_loses_exactly_the_columns_the_true_arc_reaches`: a fillet over-cut by 0.06 mm. Exactly the wall columns the true arc reaches are cut.\n- **New `sim/tests/scan_arc.rs`** (2 tests; both fail on the old code):\n  - `a_ball_rolled_round_an_outside_corner_reads_no_gouge`: the old code read 0.0092 mm of phantom gouge.\n  - `a_wall_the_true_arc_dips_into_reads_the_dip`: the old code read 0, the true value is 0.0045 mm.\n- `stock_bull_nose::both_engines_cut_a_bull_nose_alike`: back to 1e-5 parity on the arc.\n\n## Readings\n- **Debug:** all `camcraft-sim` suites pass. All `camcraft-app` and `camcraft-motion` suites pass, including the slow finish3d, pivot5d, flow and center_cut_multiaxis: 2028 passed, 0 failed.\n- **Release heavy lanes** (`--ignored`, sim + motion + app): 64 passed, 0 failed.\n- **No golden changed.**\n- **clippy:** `-p camcraft-sim --all-targets -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n- The GPU and headless UI tests: `camcraft-ui` was not touched, and its GPU headless tests are known to fail on this machine.\n- A hand check of the displayed stock in the GUI.\n- The rotary field (`field_rotary`), which has its own chord law and is untouched.\n- **Cost:** a helical piece costs about the same as the old golden-section chord (8 samples plus a 32-round golden section, against 32 rounds). A level piece costs one evaluation. Cost was not benchmarked beyond the suite times, which did not move noticeably.\n\nChangelog: The field-engine stock view and the generator-time envelope scan cut XY arcs on the true circle instead of inscribed chords, so a convex corner no longer shows a false cut and a concave corner or bull-nose helix no longer hides a real one (#413)\n\nFixes #413\nFixes #494 (the `stock_engine_agreement` heavy lane: 6465 findings on main, 0 on this head, see the comment on #494)\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
